Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), synthesized from metal ions/clusters and multitopic organic linkers, have attracted significant attention due to their exceptional internal surface area, crystallinity and tunability.1-4 These advantageous properties have been utilized for the design of functional materials including, but not limited to, adsorbents,5-11 sensors,12-13 catalysts and catalyst supports,14-16 and drug carriers.17-18 Among previously reported MOF families, $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}_{6}.$ based MOFs, constructed from $\mathrm{[Zr_{6}(\mu{-}O)_{4}(\mu{-}O H)_{4}]^{12+}}$ clusters and multitopic carboxylate-based organic linkers, exhibit remarkable structural versatility and superior stability under harsh conditions.19-21 In particular, the coordination between tetratopic linkages and $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ clusters has led to MOFs with various topologies and pore structures. Isolating pure phase MOFs over mixed products within this aforementioned family is highly dependent on the empirical manipulation of synthetic factors such as linker/node ratios, temperatures, concentrations, and modulator identity.19, 22-31 Indeed, ten different topologies have been reported from tetratopic organic linkers coordinated with $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters including ftw,23, 32-35 csq,23-24, 31, 36 she,29 shp,28 scu,25, 37 flu,38-40 ith,38 sqc,26 $\mathbf{lvt^{41}}$ and stp.42
Further increasing structural complexity, more than one topology can be accessed from the same combination of organic linkers and Zr nodes, complicating the rational design of phasepure $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}$ -MOFs for aforesaid applications.43 Within the Zr-MOF family comprised of tetratopic linkers, csq-net structures with hierarchical micro- and meso-porosity have been extensively studied for their versatile applications in particular catalysis.18, 44-46 However, the scu topology is a geometric isomer of csqnet that instead contains only diamond-shaped, microporous channels.25 An example of this topological diversity is the pair of structural isomers, csq-net NU-1000 and scu-net NU-901, that are constructed from a tetratopic pyrene-based linker (TBAPy4–, 1,3,6,8-tetrakis( $\dot{\boldsymbol{p}}$ -benzoate)pyrene) and 8- connected $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters.47 However, the underlying mechanism that dictates formation of pure-phase csq-net and scu-net from the same building units is still poorly understood.
As a dynamic microscopic process, the phase transition in MOFs has been elusive owing to experimental limitations for the observation of spatiotemporal evolution of their structures. In situ experiments are essential for the direct observation of these processes where transformations can be induced to occur with simultaneous observation. Variable temperature liquid-cell transmission electron microscopy (VT-LCTEM) is an increasingly powerful technique allowing the observation of dynamic processes of nanomaterials in situ and has been successfully utilized in studying growth mechanisms of MOFs and metal−organic nanotubes (MONTs).48-49 This promising approach has yet to be applied to gather insight on the uncommon, yet highly ambiguous, examples of phase transitions within MOFs.
Herein, we demonstrate the in situ monitoring of the polymorphic phase transition between a microporous MOF, NU-906 with a scu topology (kinetic product) , and a mesoporous MOF, NU-1008 with a csq topology (thermodynamic product),50 via in situ VT-LCTEM, highresolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) and in situ variable temperature powder X-ray diffraction (VT-PXRD).
The mesoporous csq-net MOF NU-1008,51 which is isoreticular to NU-1000, was synthesized using a 1,4-dibromo-2,3,5,6- tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)benzene $\left(\mathrm{TCPB}{\cdot}\mathrm{Br}_{2}\right)$ linker using formic acid as the modulator. In comparison, a new scu-net MOF, NU-906, was crystallized from the same building units as NU-1008 by using a different modulator, benzoic acid, in DMF (Figure 1). An oval morphology characteristic of a scu topology was observed for NU-906, as compared to rod-like crystallites expected for csq. The experimental powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern of NU-906 (Figure 2) was in good agreement with its isoreticular structure NU-904 which was determined with the reticular-merohedral twin scu topology (Figure S14),51 through which the simulated crystal structure was determined via the Rietveld refinement, and the refinement results $(R_{w}=9.0\%$ , Table S1 and Figure S13) matched well with the optimized unit cell parameters. The internal porosity was characterized by nitrogen adsorption/desorption measurements under $77~\mathrm{K}$ which yielded a type I isotherm, indicative of microporosity. NU-906 yielded a Brunauer– Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area and pore volume of 1100 $\mathbf{m}^{2}/\mathbf{g}$ and $0.52{\mathrm{~cm}}^{3}/{\mathrm{g}},$ respectively. Additionally, micropores centered at $11\textup{\AA}$ was observed in the DFT-calculated pore size distribution (Figure S3).
Inspired by the intriguing, yet poorly understood, modulatordirected topological diversity, phase transition experiments between the two MOFs were investigated. After soaking scunet NU-906 in a mixture of DMF/formic acid at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , an irreversible conversion into csq-net NU-1008 was observed. During this formic acid modulation process, NU-906 was completely dissolved to yield a clear solution followed by the precipitation of the new phase, NU-1008 (NU-906@formic acid). The transition into the csq-net was detected through the emergence of a peak at ${\sim}2.5^{\circ}$ in the PXRD pattern and a characteristic type $\mathrm{IV}$ nitrogen adsorption isotherm of NU906@formic acid (Figure 2). In addition, the phase transition only occurred in the direction of scu-net to csq-net, while the csq-net could not be transformed into the scu-net, further indicating the thermodynamic nature of the csq phase over the kinetic scu topology.43, 50, 52
Within a sealed capillary containing NU-906 and a mixture of DMF/formic acid (3:1), the phase transition was monitored through in situ VT-PXRD. The PXRD patterns were collected with a programmed temperature increased from $20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to 120 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a heating rate of $10\ {^{\circ}\mathrm{C/min}}$ . Under continuous heating from 20 to $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ an observed reduction in peak intensity at ${\sim}5^{\circ}$ (Figure 3) was attributed to a decrease in the amount of NU906 crystallites present. Starting from $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the appearance of the low angle peak at ${\sim}2.5^{\circ}$ and the now increasing intensity at ${\sim}5^{\circ}$ , suggested NU-1008 formation as both are characteristic of csq topology. The inconsistence of peaks’ relative intensity $(\sim2.5^{\circ},\sim5^{\circ})$ between in situ and bulk experiment could be attributed to overlapping of the peak at $4.91^{\circ}$ in NU-1008 with the first peak of NU-906 at $5^{\circ}$ due to incomplete dissolution of NU-906 during the formation of the new phase NU-1008 in the confined space of capillary.
The phase transition from NU-906 to NU-1008 over time was examined through high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) (Figure 4). TEM images of NU-906 (Figure 4a,b) revealed the oval-shaped morphology of the crystals and lattice spacing of ${\sim}1.7~\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The rod-shape morphology crystals were solely observed after two hours with lattice spacing of ${\sim}3.5~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 4c,d), suggesting that NU
906 undergoes a phase change(s) to NU-1008. When the reaction time was extended, the initially crystallized NU-1008 grew to larger crystals after two days (Figure 4e,f).
