The spatiotemporal evolution process of the phase transition of NU-906 to NU-1008 was directly monitored by in situ LCTEM measurements (Figure 5). NU-906 particles suspended in methanol were drop-casted onto a bottom chip and allowed to dry before the chips were assembled. A mixture of DMF and formic acid solution was introduced into the dry cell with NU906 particles inside. Once the wetting of the liquid cell was confirmed, the liquid cell was heated to $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (below the boiling point of formic acid). Firstly, we performed the in situ experiments without exposure to the electron beam, confirming that the confined liquid-cell environment would not affect the transition of NU-906 to NU-1008 particles (see SI, section 5). Additionally, an electron flux threshold was established to ensure observations were performed without beam induced damage effects. Following several optimization experiments to determine electron flux with enough contrast, we confirmed safe imaging conditions as being $<6\mathrm{\e}^{-\8-2}$ cumulative electron flux (Figure S10). Hence, to minimize beam effects on the system, time lapse images were recorded every $5{\cdot}10\ \mathrm{min}$ with an exposure time of 0.25 sec rather than via a continuous acquisition protocol. This approach serves to keep the cumulative electron flux well below $6\mathrm{e}^{-\bigl\/-2}$ .
The dissolution of NU-906 was observed for up to $10\ \mathrm{min}$ (Figure 5a-d) in a DMF: formic acid $=7{:}1$ solution. Through further monitoring of the phase transition, the formation of NU-1008 did not occur under these conditions. By performing a similar experiment with a higher concentration of formic acid (DMF: formic acid $=3{:}1$ ), the initial dissolution of NU-906 was too fast to be captured (Figure 5e,f); a process also observed as rapid even at room temperature as per in situ PXRD experiments. Particle formation was detected after $10{-}20~\mathrm{min}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 5g,h), which led to the observation of rodshaped NU-1008 particles with lattice spacing of ${\sim}3.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ during post-mortem (dry-state imaging after the in situ experiment) analysis (Figure 5i,j). These in situ LCTEM experiments show that the phase transition follows a dissolution-reprecipitation mechanism. That is, formic acid facilitates the dissolution of NU-906 then acts as a modulator for the formation of the second phase, NU-1008. The dissolution of NU-906 results in an intermediate solution containing $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ clusters, TCPB- ${\bf\cdot B r}_{2}$ linker, excess formic acid and DMF. This solution has the same constituents as the starting synthetic solution that yields NU-1008. Therefore, dissolution of NU-906 generates NU-1008 concomitantly. Within the bulk experiment, the observation of a clear solution generated after NU-906 was dissolved into DMF/formic acid (3:1), after a few minutes at $80\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ supports this growth process. The clear solution formed from dissolved NU-906 could be directly flowed into the liquid cell and heated to $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ wherein rodshaped particles formed after $30\mathrm{min}$ which were confirmed to be NU-1008 during post-mortem analysis, further supporting the dissolution-reprecipitation mechanism (Figure S11). For liquid cell experiment in a DMF: formic acid $=5{:}1$ solution, presence of particles was observed during the whole process, and the amount of particles remained dynamic. Both NU-906 (lattice spacing of ${\sim}1.7~\mathrm{nm}$ ) and NU-1008 (lattice spacing of ${\sim}3.5\mathrm{~\nm}]$ ) were detected during post-mortem analysis, indicating both dissolution and reprecipitation processes happened concurrently under this condition(Figure S12).
This study provides a correlation between MOF topologies and experimental parameters through the synthesis of two phasepure MOFs with different topologies built from identical building units yet different modulators. A phase transition from the bulk, pure phase scu-NU-906 yielded new phase csqNU-1008, characterized through PXRD and nitrogen isotherms. Moreover, a combination of in situ characterization techniques, VT-PXRD, HRTEM and VT-LCTEM, investigated the mechanism of the dynamic MOF phase transition. These methodologies suggested that the phase transition from scu-NU-906 to csqNU-1008 occurs through a dissolution-reprecipitation mechanism. Future work will focus on methods to improve spatiotemporal resolution of VT-LCTEM, ultimately providing more detailed mechanistic studies on dynamic processes of beam-sensitive materials. We anticipate, and propose that the combination of these techniques will be broadly applicable, providing mechanistic insights of dynamic processes within crystalline materials generally.
